6. Comparison Between Project 3A and Project 3B
The main difference between the two experiments is whether the pretrained Word2Vec embeddings are frozen or updated during classifier training.
Metric
Project 3A: Frozen
Project 3B: Trainable
Mean CV Accuracy
58.79%
65.67%
Mean CV Macro F1
0.4945
0.6207
Test Accuracy
56.80%
55.19%
Test Macro F1
0.5358
0.5468

Project 3B achieved a higher average cross-validation accuracy and macro F1 score than Project 3A. Its test macro F1 score was also slightly higher.
However, Project 3A achieved a slightly higher test accuracy, with 56.80% compared with 55.19% for Project 3B.
These results suggest that allowing Word2Vec embeddings to be fine-tuned can improve the model's ability to distinguish between sentiment categories. However, the improvement in cross-validation performance did not translate into higher overall test accuracy.
7. Discussion
The experiments demonstrate the impact of embedding fine-tuning on Twitter sentiment classification.
In Project 3A, the pretrained Word2Vec embeddings remained unchanged during classifier training. Although the classifier learned to use these representations, the word vectors could not adapt to the specific sentiment classification task.
In Project 3B, the embedding weights were updated during training. This allowed the model to adjust word representations based on the sentiment labels. The improved cross-validation results suggest that this flexibility helped the model learn more task-specific representations.
Nevertheless, Project 3B did not achieve higher test accuracy. One possible explanation is overfitting, since the training loss continued to decrease while validation performance peaked earlier and fluctuated afterward.
The dataset also contains an uneven distribution of sentiment labels. The neutral class has more training examples than the negative class, which may affect the model's ability to classify each sentiment equally well.
8. Conclusion
This project implemented two neural network models for three-class Twitter sentiment classification using pretrained Word2Vec embeddings.
Project 3A used frozen embeddings, while Project 3B allowed the embeddings to be fine-tuned during training.
Project 3B achieved better average cross-validation results and a slightly higher test macro F1 score. However, Project 3A achieved higher test accuracy.
Overall, the results show that fine-tuning pretrained word embeddings can improve sentiment classification performance, particularly when evaluating performance across multiple classes. However, higher validation and cross-validation scores do not necessarily guarantee better performance on unseen test data.
Future improvements could include adjusting the learning rate, applying dropout or other regularization methods, handling class imbalance, and experimenting with different neural network architectures.

